#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119640 :question/id #uuid "ba897e22-8104-5772-a0d3-9b062f3592f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4932" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119641 :question/id #uuid "a33f6c43-4e25-5d67-9f73-ca99a18fb434" :question/math-academy-id "e-4933" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3.$ What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle.$ So, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set $t_1=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any $t_3 \\neq 0.$ For example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119642 :question/id #uuid "43586a83-879c-5caf-9063-a8f4afbb8b4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4935" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ y &=-1+\\sqrt{3}t \\\\ z &=5-t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 3 \\\\ y = - 1 + \\sqrt{3} t \\\\ z = 5 - t\\end{array} \\right. } \\qquad  \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\n\n\\langle x, y, z \\rangle= \\langle 3, -1 , 5 \\rangle\n+t \\ \\underbrace{\\langle 0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1 \\rangle}_{\\large \\mathbf{v}} .\n$$\n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1\\rangle,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2},0,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119643 :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119644 :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119645 :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119646 :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119647 :question/id #uuid "a6fded96-287a-53ee-8b1c-dd618562b2c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4940" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$  and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle,$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-3,2,5⟩+s\\,⟨17,3,15⟩+t\\,⟨5,0,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119648 :question/id #uuid "297bda8f-6e33-5cc2-a2e1-5ab6186fa9d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4941" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(2,50,-19)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}= \\langle 2,\\sqrt{6},17 \\rangle + s \\langle -1,7,2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{r}= \\langle 20,-44, 27 \\rangle + t \\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle  -1,7,2  \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(2,50,-19)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,50,-19 \\rangle.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨2,50,-19⟩+s\\,⟨-1,7,2⟩+t\\,⟨2\\sqrt{3},9,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119649 :question/id #uuid "9c36ddd5-fcf1-5c4d-a350-aa8ef3a3ca9e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4942" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(-1,4,-3),$ $B(7,5,4),$ and $C(2,-2,8).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨7,5,4⟩-⟨-1,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,1,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2,-2,8⟩-⟨-1,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -1,4,-3 \\rangle$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s\\overset{\\to }{AB}+t\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\ r & =⟨-1,4,-3⟩+s⟨8,1,7⟩+t⟨3,-6,11⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The form of the final equation depends on our choice of vectors and the point on the plane. We could have picked other vectors. Likewise, we could have chosen $B$ or $C$ instead of $A$ as our point on the plane. So, the answer is *not unique*!"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119664 :question/id #uuid "b86eace4-01cb-5601-8e9b-2658e81fc656" :question/math-academy-id "e-5884" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i-12j-20k \\\\  & =⟨1,-12,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e448815-b2bb-5846-915b-7d9c23884bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390830} {:answer/id #uuid "de65980c-0a80-5e6f-bf87-e8afefe3f527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=⟨-2,5,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390831} {:answer/id #uuid "2f57365b-0f99-52f4-983d-60b6ccab865c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-5,3⟩=⟨-1,2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390832} {:answer/id #uuid "687ca452-cc51-531c-b86f-23343784df77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390833} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdfb2c3-82ee-5d77-b2ad-4381c658ae0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-5,3⟩=21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390830} :answer-field/id #uuid "178ed90e-697e-5682-a979-46b601de175d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "965706e8-86b7-56ea-92d3-d6b5a93e54f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-32971" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(2,-5,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-5,3⟩,$ and $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =⟨2,-5,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b98d4014-27ab-5ffc-a9a1-94f2d76d7cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨3, 1, 5⟩ + s⟨3,-9,-5⟩ + t⟨ - 2, 1, 3⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117470} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe44dd9-7bdd-5239-bc90-bb3f3d5c8dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117471} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d68798-b39f-5fd9-ad83-ef58d2f0912b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨3, 1, 5⟩ + s⟨3,-9,-5⟩ + t⟨ - 5, 0,-2⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117472} {:answer/id #uuid "fd46fa5e-f228-5b17-ab87-937c2c49b057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨3, 1, 5⟩ + s⟨6,-8, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 2, 1, 3⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117473} {:answer/id #uuid "164382bd-289b-5666-97e1-536a448ecc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨3, 1, 5⟩ + s⟨6,-8, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 5, 0,-2⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117474}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b6cca1ea-c183-5baf-841b-897aa164800b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00e2b8e-5c43-5f1e-861a-b8621cd03fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390836} {:answer/id #uuid "d0576584-3750-547a-9169-cba885383592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨3,-9,-5⟩+t⟨-2,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390837} {:answer/id #uuid "cc58ca2d-f965-5a26-a6a7-4b5c5d9d1c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨6,-8,0⟩+t⟨-5,0,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390838} {:answer/id #uuid "448cf521-8dea-57c9-a228-ac326b781ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨3,-9,-5⟩+t⟨-5,0,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390839} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fdd784-d294-5584-81fc-4a5e521dbe8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨6,-8,0⟩+t⟨-2,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390839} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f59cecd-7538-5379-bf6c-ff75d0435b9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "26d7019c-08c2-5f92-a7e5-2893b8366abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32988" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(3,1,5),\\,B(6,-8,0)$, and $C(-2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨6,-8,0⟩-⟨3,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-9,-5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨-2,1,3⟩-⟨3,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $a=⟨3,1,5⟩$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s\\overset{\\to }{AB}+t\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨3,-9,-5⟩+t⟨-5,0,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39cb606-c863-581e-9ff9-2728265a795f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117477} {:answer/id #uuid "54e5a00f-239b-539d-9f9b-5957f11c633b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨6,0,2⟩+s⟨3,1,-2⟩+t⟨6,0,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489715} {:answer/id #uuid "6842561b-d1ec-5753-b48f-a0a96c8d26d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨6,0,2⟩+s⟨6,0,2⟩+t⟨1,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489762} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea6b719-d65e-5d67-8999-88b13c999f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨6,0,2⟩+s⟨3,1,-2⟩+t⟨1,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489776} {:answer/id #uuid "e994da9e-a986-53ac-b530-86db42005ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨6,0,2⟩+s⟨3,1,-2⟩+t⟨-5,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490021} :answer-field/id #uuid "54de0330-8cb1-5db4-9316-aaffcc0d2f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71d2a1f8-b97c-598b-abe7-896238f620c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-32989" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(6,0,2)$ and $B(1,1,3)$, and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1,3⟩-⟨6,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $a=⟨6,0,2⟩$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\ r & =⟨6,0,2⟩+s⟨3,1,-2⟩+t⟨-5,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1490998e-fd96-558a-92bf-90c3e1b1d823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k)=2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442986} {:answer/id #uuid "24bd5c51-ea5f-55c2-b7cc-0a1c6fdaf3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442987} {:answer/id #uuid "26e81e0f-00be-5150-9e74-fec4066ead13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442988} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a2f39f-065a-5b9a-9af2-2369637cc071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442989} {:answer/id #uuid "eead13a6-edac-5ccb-bd23-f3ae56dce20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442988} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea9a67f-f999-5059-be26-16de635db5bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3aea6384-95f5-5566-a595-7a25ca576605" :question/math-academy-id "q-33029" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(\\sqrt{2},3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k,$ and $n=2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k) & =(\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k)\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k) \\\\ r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8f836e-b1bd-5929-a0d4-cc62e2406547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420147} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6228-4151-5fb7-a8b8-4defb3112da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420148} {:answer/id #uuid "ab54b251-064b-57d4-8fc1-3a45c6f498e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=⟨-2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420149} {:answer/id #uuid "b05ca4a9-4343-56a3-86ae-980793c8602f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩=⟨2,-3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420150} {:answer/id #uuid "f59eba3d-6e2f-504f-905a-a227d63c2c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2947f04-3e78-507e-b09a-00ccebdf73ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee828cf7-8b80-575c-a644-51d699464b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-33037" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,-3,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,-3,5⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,2,-5⟩-⟨3,2,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-2,0,3⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd010f61-f497-566f-ac0f-c50c695f2870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390876} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fe09e5-ac83-5e62-97ff-7cc91f6b67c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨0,6,2⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨6,-5,10⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390877} {:answer/id #uuid "5b991d71-53b1-55cf-8e90-ebe111f71f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨8,1,0⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390878} {:answer/id #uuid "c86fee90-6ade-5c0b-9714-4278894c742b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨8,1,0⟩+t⟨6,-5,10⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390879} {:answer/id #uuid "db01346f-8d56-5827-aa82-3725170f51a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390880} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf886e4d-3534-5b42-92f4-88d2e9d1dbe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0602f3f6-acc2-53ef-a72e-b71caa5d5289" :question/math-academy-id "q-33066" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(5,-3,1)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨8,1,0⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩$ and $r=⟨6,-5,10⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,6,2⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(5,-3,1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $a=⟨5,-3,1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6efa4f-103e-5d09-a0b9-0000c80abb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x + 2y - 5z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117546} {:answer/id #uuid "db3b50b9-abd4-5dba-9e03-c0800bb6e138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 2y + 5z + 7 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117547} {:answer/id #uuid "0eeb1aa7-6dff-58c8-b5b2-671e63d7f41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + y - 4z - 19 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117548} {:answer/id #uuid "1d92a7bb-99c9-50e9-9fde-aaa8bacc8638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + y - 4z - 7 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117549} {:answer/id #uuid "79474bef-c5ed-50b0-8a84-65f7dc6ddb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 2y + 5z + 19 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117550}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c3139a7d-3108-5452-8a92-6fc4ea874118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117545}] :question/id #uuid "ef5e0514-3a0a-5e4f-ae1e-e23afafac839" :question/math-academy-id "q-33139" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3, 1,-4)$ and is perpendicular to the 