/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d3ff8e-37f2-5365-be21-7d8861823943" :question/math-academy-id "q-35595" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956a28c1-88c4-5aa1-b716-262ebc42d285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192226} {:answer/id #uuid "ad90e3b3-269c-563b-b71f-24bc245a5e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192227} {:answer/id #uuid "f6054008-de6c-5176-a4c9-28dfd98b924a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192228} {:answer/id #uuid "24dc6385-472c-5a27-b1c4-1ce851510fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192229} {:answer/id #uuid "7179dc64-8183-521f-9583-2a1a3db8bf96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192230}] :answer-field/id #uuid "03175621-f90f-5d80-8450-8dcdeb1aed67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27e9ecca-4f5e-5a05-9b68-a788e860e9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-74215" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9b2c979ee7356d3c8b638ad46cef3733d6c0db70965f1deec24413c5bf2136e.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $R^{2}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f169903-2deb-58c4-8e5e-f18aebb5bb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192232} {:answer/id #uuid "e863eea8-0bc1-59a9-925c-9c4c728425e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192233} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca3740b-cad7-5153-9f5b-c014086802fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192234} {:answer/id #uuid "126391f6-93c1-5848-b8d4-c91335d5226c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192235} {:answer/id #uuid "623a3779-f921-588b-b02a-0335991327ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192236}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a7516bfd-9386-5de7-8f78-9daeb00b77e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ca2a0cb-ac20-5824-b2b0-2f129bd35e1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74216" :question/problem "![](images/15/15da2a7bcb642ecc6f5e8d584e89bee3d1002e6f5e31c85b27db0b337553ebbb.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $R^{3}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198151 :question/id #uuid "06ee94f5-8d8c-537b-83ff-69d6e351e4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13145" :question/problem "![](images/89/89fa15ffb9a7f6b319cf3b9ff0de4ab821a15f632d7f93618fbe99b7979260f4.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation." :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{ -3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{ (x,y)  \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\: : \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}  \n\\leq  x \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3} , \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}  \n\\leq  y \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \n\\Big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198152 :question/id #uuid "487a42f5-a562-5df1-9225-dbbc0046c002" :question/math-academy-id "e-13156" :question/problem "![](images/45/45260087c5196cc239f21402d73169f60139bd7d2c10ee0aa1db4e1a2c56be4a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2 } \\: : \\:  2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt } y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3 \\Big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198153 :question/id #uuid "88fba9fd-d05b-546c-89aa-edcf8d2fc93e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13158" :question/problem "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f5f2f2e67ba0ad566b2759ca5536d248f671bf0f082d567a7f7d993772601e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\gt 400.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 }x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25 }y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400 \\bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198163 :question/id #uuid "56561d78-d192-5197-81f7-303abf75018f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13183" :question/problem "![](images/78/787b62fbe367feae9e4c8ed10b983a256840e3a93727fea7c1ff67cacad0129d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x+2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\: : \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f930f1e0-0d30-5aa9-b5fd-483a8a5c9a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389863} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2d7141-7e67-5310-b9fb-4911d2b63443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389864} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad9e1f0-b0b4-597f-90f2-178c494264ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389865} {:answer